From 34a977e3cbb0852420e0b1bcdf5b2375bc12ac53 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kristian <kristian@apache.org>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 03:10:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Format protobuf client values without java.time and
 BigInteger

The client turns dates, times, timestamps and decimals into the text
the json encoding carries, and on a type-rich result that made it
decode slower than json. Write each value into a byte array of its
exact length and make a Latin-1 string of it: the civil date comes
from the epoch day arithmetically, a decimal of up to 128 bits is
converted by dividing its words, and only a wider one goes through
BigInteger. The formats are pinned to LocalDate, DateTimeFormatter and
BigDecimal by tests over the ends of each range and random values.

A column of timestamps with time zone usually holds one zone, so the
decoder keeps the previous zone id and reuses it when the bytes match,
instead of decoding the text for every value.

On the generated type-rich table of BenchmarkQueryDataEncoding the
protobuf decode goes from 1.7x slower than json to 1.2x slower.
